{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims the Decoy Perturbation is the ONLY single-evaluation evidence distinguishing reliability from compliance, but this is false: cross-validation of reasoning traces offers a second, distinct single-evaluation signal. When a non-expert panel arrives at the objectively correct answer, one can examine whether each member's checklist annotations contain task-relevant justifications that coherently map to the verified outcome versus mere item-by-item 'pass' markings with no mechanistic linkage to the answer. A genuinely reliable panel produces calibrated reasoning artifacts (e.g., flagging specific evidence for each check, noting uncertainty); a merely compliant panel produces uniform, content-free affirmations. This reasoning-trace audit occurs within the same single evaluation and does not require inserting a logically irrelevant or contradictory requirement, yet it still separates reliability from compliance.","counterexample":[{"evaluation_design":"Within one evaluation, for each panel that produces the verified-correct outcome, extract every member's per-item checklist annotation. Code annotations as 'substantive' if they cite task-specific evidence or mechanistic reasoning linking the check to the outcome, and 'procedural' if they contain only generic confirmations ('pass', 'done') with no outcome-linked justification.","prediction":"Two panels both achieve 100% correct answers. Panel A's annotations are uniformly substantive (cite evidence, note uncertainty per item). Panel B's annotations are uniformly procedural (only 'pass' marks). Panel A is reliable; Panel B is compliant-yet-correct — and this distinction is obtained without any decoy item, refuting the claim that the decoy is the only distinguishing evidence within a single evaluation."}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues the panel is more reliable because the checklist's completeness constraint mitigates the expert's premature closure, but its own countercondition (checklist <80% coverage) already concedes the mechanism can fail. More critically, the argument never establishes that the checklist-driven 'higher floor' yields superior AVERAGE reliability rather than merely a narrower variance band. A single expert's intuition, despite premature-closure risk, can recover via self-correction on verifiable tasks (the expert can check their own answer against the objective outcome and revise), whereas a non-expert panel locked into an incomplete or mis-specified checklist may systematically converge on a wrong answer with high confidence and no self-correction capacity. The argument conflates 'cannot skip steps' with 'more reliable on average,' but preventing step-skipping in a flawed checklist can lock in a lower performance ceiling that drags the mean below the expert's, exactly within the stated countercondition space the target does not rule out.","counterexample":[{"task":"A verifiable diagnostic task with a 70%-coverage checklist that omits one critical but non-obvious step.","panel_outcome":"The panel, unable to skip or question checklist items, completes all listed checks, marks 'pass,' and converges on the wrong diagnosis with high confidence — mean accuracy 0.70.","expert_outcome":"The expert, using intuition, prematurely closes on the wrong answer on some cases but can self-correct by testing against the objective outcome, achieving mean accuracy 0.80.","result":"The completeness constraint does not produce higher average reliability; it produces a lower floor than the expert's self-correcting intuition under a partially-specified checklist, within the countercondition space SRC_002 acknowledges but does not exclude."}]}]}